he turned out to find one.

00:12:03.540 --> 00:12:06.931
So actually it's been really useful,
and that I think is

00:12:06.931 --> 00:12:12.030
the foremost important thing of a Stanford
network, and it's very closely knit.

00:12:12.030 --> 00:12:14.760
So it's really easy to
reach out to someone.

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:17.442
You really know the person,
it's not a large class.

00:12:17.442 --> 00:12:22.360
You end up knowing maybe 80,
85% of your class, you really know them.

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:23.891
So it's really good.

00:12:23.891 --> 00:12:28.684
And then the second thing I would say,
for me, the most instrumental class so

00:12:28.684 --> 00:12:32.100
far is still Touchy Feely or
interpersonal dynamics.

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:36.046
I think that's a class
that I still use daily.

00:12:36.046 --> 00:12:40.615
I actually recently bought the book
that came our of the course, and

00:12:40.615 --> 00:12:44.703
really helped me [INAUDIBLE] and
remember all the concepts and

00:12:44.703 --> 00:12:47.769
all the learnings that
I had from that class.

00:12:47.769 --> 00:12:51.771
But I would say, for me,
still the most valuable class, and

00:12:51.771 --> 00:12:56.574
it sounds cliche because most of
alums say that, but it's really true.

00:12:59.504 --> 00:13:04.949
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I will mention,
in my case I knew I had, similar to Diego,

00:13:04.949 --> 00:13:10.803
I had actually ten years in
the United States before starting the GSB.

00:13:10.803 --> 00:13:13.293
And I knew I wanted to
return to Latin America.

00:13:13.293 --> 00:13:18.050
So a lot of my GSB
experiences were around that.

00:13:18.050 --> 00:13:22.129
So I did a GMIX in Paraguay.

00:13:22.129 --> 00:13:24.540
My GSB was in Chile.

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:27.900
And then I led a GST to Panama and
Honduras.

00:13:27.900 --> 00:13:32.500
So I knew that I wanted to make
sure that I had the knowledge of

00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:34.770
the business culture.

00:13:34.770 --> 00:13:37.593
Anything that I needed to do in
Latin America because most of

00:13:37.593 --> 00:13:40.140
my professional life I had
been in the United States.

00:13:40.140 --> 00:13:44.078
So I took advantage of those two years
to make sure that I reconnected with

00:13:44.078 --> 00:13:45.420
Latin America.

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:51.169
And I also would like to highlight
what Alice said, is the network.

00:13:51.169 --> 00:13:56.042
I pretty much know all of
the Latin American people in my class and

00:13:56.042 --> 00:13:59.664
you get to meet the ones above,
the ones below.

00:13:59.664 --> 00:14:04.241
But in particular, since I knew
that I wanted to return to Mexico,

00:14:04.241 --> 00:14:09.170
I took the time to create and
build those strong relationships.

00:14:09.170 --> 00:14:12.580
And most of my job that I do
right now is fundraising.

00:14:12.580 --> 00:14:17.353
And thanks to the network of the GSB,
I'm able to achieve the goals that I

00:14:17.353 --> 00:14:20.570
need to do for fundraising and
teach for Mexico.

00:14:20.570 --> 00:14:24.991
So I definitely see it as a place
where you will create these

00:14:24.991 --> 00:14:28.700
awesome relationships
that are very intimate.

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:31.634
So whenever you would reach out to them,

00:14:31.634 --> 00:14:35.530
they'll say yes to whatever
you're asking of them.

00:14:35.530 --> 00:14:39.742
So those are the things that
I would like to highlight.

00:14:39.742 --> 00:14:41.663
Excellent, and just for our audience,

00:14:41.663 --> 00:14:44.160
there were a couple of
acronyms you threw out there.

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:47.790
So GMIX, and I think GST.

00:14:47.790 --> 00:14:51.751
&gt;&gt; Global Management Immersion Experience,
which is for

00:14:51.751 --> 00:14:57.550
four weeks you get to go and work in
a country where you have never worked.

00:14:57.550 --> 00:15:01.227
And a GST, a global study trip.

00:15:01.227 --> 00:15:04.380
The one that I did in Chile
was as a participant.

00:15:04.380 --> 00:15:09.312
So it's a trip in which you go for
a full week to talk to business people,

00:15:09.312 --> 00:15:14.082
to people from the cultural aspect
to actually know what it's like do

00:15:14.082 --> 00:15:16.390
business in that country.

00:15:16.390 --> 00:15:20.058
And you also, as a second year,
you can lead a trips.

00:15:20.058 --> 00:15:24.008
And it is awesome because you
get to talk to express events,

00:15:24.008 --> 00:15:28.120
quorum president when you look
into organize this meetings.

00:15:28.120 --> 00:15:33.047
But I definitely recommend them
because it truly allows you to see,

00:15:33.047 --> 00:15:36.256
even though it's only seven or eight days.

00:15:36.256 --> 00:15:41.019
We'd really pack those experiences and
I definitely recommend them.

00:15:44.917 --> 00:15:46.240
&gt;&gt; Excellent, thank you.

00:15:48.831 --> 00:15:55.528
&gt;&gt; I don't think I took any classes
that were focused on Latin America.

00:15:55.528 --> 00:16:00.549
I would definitely echo
the The alumni network, professors

00:16:00.549 --> 00:16:05.777
are more than an admissions
officer told me that

00:16:05.777 --> 00:16:11.536
they were the most underutilized
assets at the GSB.

00:16:11.536 --> 00:16:17.419
Most professors there are top of
their industry, their total badasses.

00:16:17.419 --> 00:16:19.872
So, professors alarms,

00:16:19.872 --> 00:16:25.669
almost like any class that
touches on interpersonal skills,

00:16:25.669 --> 00:16:30.464
communication skills,
relationship building,

00:16:30.464 --> 00:16:36.604
all of that ended up being super
helpful to build my own networks.

00:16:36.604 --> 00:16:41.592
And for me also the entrepreneurship
classes like startup

00:16:41.592 --> 00:16:46.188
garage really helped me
shift my internal chip from.

