00:29:50.661 --> 00:29:56.853
A few of our attendees are wondering
what was your most memorable

00:29:56.853 --> 00:30:02.136
experience during the,
during your MBA experience?

00:30:02.136 --> 00:30:07.856
So most memorable experience or
the most valuable impact on you,

00:30:07.856 --> 00:30:13.059
personally and
professionally from your MBA experience.

00:30:16.862 --> 00:30:19.436
&gt;&gt; Most memorable I'll say TALK.

00:30:19.436 --> 00:30:21.980
TALK is a tradition at the GSB.

00:30:21.980 --> 00:30:26.702
Where every Monday or Wednesday depending
on the class that you are a there

00:30:26.702 --> 00:30:28.997
whether your first or second year.

00:30:28.997 --> 00:30:31.459
Classmates share their life story.

00:30:31.459 --> 00:30:36.005
And it can be about
anything that they decide.

00:30:36.005 --> 00:30:40.212
And for me, I was able to share
what I can from, my love for

00:30:40.212 --> 00:30:42.339
education and everything.

00:30:42.339 --> 00:30:49.644
And remembering, seen so many people just
curious to know who I was as a person.

00:30:49.644 --> 00:30:52.464
And then also thinking of the other side,

00:30:52.464 --> 00:30:55.696
listening to all the stories
of my classmates.

00:30:55.696 --> 00:31:00.511
And it makes you realize how human we
are of course we are all human but

00:31:00.511 --> 00:31:02.708
when you go to those talks you,

00:31:02.708 --> 00:31:08.391
people that you've never expected to
have gone through very hard challenges.

00:31:08.391 --> 00:31:13.856
They actually went through very difficult
challenges, people that you never knew

00:31:13.856 --> 00:31:18.791
that were very good at a certain sport or
very good about a certain subject.

00:31:18.791 --> 00:31:19.576
That is the most memorable.

00:31:19.576 --> 00:31:23.071
And the most impact is
definitely I will also

00:31:23.071 --> 00:31:27.420
highlight what it was has
been said about leadership.

00:31:27.420 --> 00:31:30.940
One class that has had the most
impact in my career and

00:31:30.940 --> 00:31:36.635
since I started out in Singapore, Michigan
has been managing growing enterprise.

00:31:36.635 --> 00:31:42.302
By this class, I never thought that I will
have to deal with writing a press release.

00:31:42.302 --> 00:31:46.710
I never thought that I will have to
deal with drama between founders and

00:31:46.710 --> 00:31:47.766
board members.

00:31:47.766 --> 00:31:50.341
I never thought many things at this class.

00:31:50.341 --> 00:31:55.061
I learned,
now I'm looking back in retrospect and

00:31:55.061 --> 00:32:00.774
be Literally,
they teach you many of the pain

00:32:00.774 --> 00:32:06.192
points of a growing enterprise and
prepare you for

00:32:06.192 --> 00:32:12.778
what you're going to be facing
as a leader of an organization.

00:32:12.778 --> 00:32:16.218
&gt;&gt; Yeah, my most memorable experience is,

00:32:16.218 --> 00:32:20.953
I did an exchange with
the Tsinghua University in China.

00:32:20.953 --> 00:32:24.229
And I have never been to China and
I think we went there and

00:32:24.229 --> 00:32:26.475
I think it was in October or November.

00:32:26.475 --> 00:32:30.863
It was really, really cold and
I ended up having a bronchitis and

00:32:30.863 --> 00:32:34.284
I ended up in the hospital in China,
it was crazy.

00:32:34.284 --> 00:32:39.797
But that experience was for me and
studying at the University of Tsinghua.

00:32:39.797 --> 00:32:43.210
Doing that was also really,
really impactful for me.

00:32:43.210 --> 00:32:47.544
And then when the Tsinghua students
came back to Stanford, and

00:32:47.544 --> 00:32:52.550
we got to like, they stayed at our houses,
it was a really cool program.

00:32:52.550 --> 00:32:54.093
I really enjoyed that.

00:32:54.093 --> 00:32:58.607
And then personally, I think another
really memorable experience for me was

00:32:58.607 --> 00:33:03.270
just, I went to the GSB with my daughter,
so she was three when we got to the GSB.

00:33:03.270 --> 00:33:08.136
And it was just me and her and I think,
yeah, we spent two years there and

00:33:08.136 --> 00:33:12.924
seeing her grow up and I mean grew up
because she was five when we left but

00:33:12.924 --> 00:33:16.604
grow through that process and
living there with her.

00:33:16.604 --> 00:33:22.910
And seeing her really involved in the
Stanford GSB life was really impactful.

00:33:22.910 --> 00:33:27.940
And to this day, I think it really had
a major impact on her and her development.

00:33:27.940 --> 00:33:29.256
So yeah.

00:33:31.264 --> 00:33:31.940
&gt;&gt; That's beautiful.

00:33:31.940 --> 00:33:36.269
Thanks for sharing.

00:33:36.269 --> 00:33:41.777
&gt;&gt; For me,
I remember it wasn't just one moment,

00:33:41.777 --> 00:33:48.801
but I remember being struck by
how consistent the theories,

00:33:48.801 --> 00:33:53.484
the philosophies, the evidence were,

00:33:53.484 --> 00:34:01.000
all in the direction of
a particular kind of leadership.

00:34:01.000 --> 00:34:04.744
Fortunately, was connected
to just be a better person.

00:34:04.744 --> 00:34:06.990
Listen, be humble.

00:34:06.990 --> 00:34:10.455
Try to do what's best for
your team, your customers.

00:34:10.455 --> 00:34:12.987
When you're negotiating,
it's not about crushing.

00:34:12.987 --> 00:34:18.171
It's about finding a creative win win for

00:34:18.171 --> 00:34:21.140
both parties and so on.

00:34:22.290 --> 00:34:26.552
I think only one class talked more about,
by the way,

00:34:26.552 --> 00:34:31.589
don't forget about power and
it's a classical path to power.

00:34:31.589 --> 00:34:34.529
That is kind of like
the dark side of class but

00:34:34.529 --> 00:34:38.170
everything else was very much aligned.

00:34:38.170 --> 00:34:41.528
And for me, that was helpful because
there's so many books out there, so

00:34:41.528 --> 00:34:43.605
many gurus, so many podcasts and articles.

00:34:43.605 --> 00:34:48.802
That, for me,
the consistency from very successful,

00:34:48.802 --> 00:34:53.226
very thoughtful people
backed also by research

00:34:53.226 --> 00:34:57.220
that there is this consistent narrative.

00:34:57.220 --> 00:35:00.190
It just helped me cut through so
much noise.

00:35:02.030 --> 00:35:07.144
And echoing what Pamela was saying,
I found that the hardest

00:35:07.144 --> 00:35:13.674
challenges in this line of work have
been interpersonal and intrapersonal.

00:35:13.674 --> 00:35:15.800
So I've enjoyed the GSB's emphasis, but

00:35:15.800 --> 00:35:18.110
there's a lot of classes
you can take on both.

00:35:18.110 --> 00:35:21.120
I wouldn't say there's just
like the one class and

00:35:21.120 --> 00:35:23.780
those have been the most
impactful post GSB.

00:35:27.240 --> 00:35:30.985
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you all for
sharing those reflections.

00:35:30.985 --> 00:35:39.350
There are a few questions as well about
what surprised you all about the GSB?

00:35:39.350 --> 00:35:45.720
What did you maybe not know that
you learned during your time?

00:35:45.720 --> 00:35:52.082
Or what did you not know about the GSB
that you learned during your time there?

00:35:52.082 --> 00:35:53.200
Yeah, let's start there.

00:35:53.200 --> 00:35:54.875
What surprised you about the GSB?

00:35:58.809 --> 00:36:03.488
Or even something that you wish you
had known prior to your experience.

00:36:12.436 --> 00:36:14.402
&gt;&gt; I said it earlier but

00:36:14.402 --> 00:36:20.310
I wish I had known just the caliber
of some of the professors.

00:36:21.970 --> 00:36:26.432
Yeah, I mean, there's just so
much wisdom and,

00:36:26.432 --> 00:36:31.090
networks and
resources behind each one of them.

00:36:31.090 --> 00:36:33.843
And if you try to get to know
them outside of the GSB,

00:36:33.843 --> 00:36:36.548
there's 1000 steps you
have to get through.

00:36:36.548 --> 00:36:40.092
At GSB you can just take their class, you
can take them out for coffee and so on.

00:36:40.092 --> 00:36:44.233
So just investing a little bit more time
not just reading the class reviews, or

00:36:44.233 --> 00:36:47.747
not just reading the class description but
also trying to be like,

00:36:47.747 --> 00:36:49.209
who are these professors?

00:36:49.209 --> 00:36:53.685
And trying to build those personal
connections with them and

00:36:53.685 --> 00:36:55.710
that they are responsive.

00:36:55.710 --> 00:36:59.500
They will grab coffee with you,
if you're their students, mostly.

00:36:59.500 --> 00:37:04.581
I found that surprising and I wish I had
be even more intentional about that and

00:37:04.581 --> 00:37:06.387
carved more time for that.

00:37:13.323 --> 00:37:18.000
&gt;&gt; I wish I will have known how intense
it is, because I mean we are talking from

00:37:18.000 --> 00:37:21.664
the place that we finish and
we love it and everything.

00:37:21.664 --> 00:37:26.241
But be prepared for a place where if
you want to make your experience more

00:37:26.241 --> 00:37:30.052
enjoyable, it took me the first
quarter to realize that.

00:37:30.052 --> 00:37:36.439
You cannot do everything,
all the opportunities out there.

00:37:36.439 --> 00:37:40.140
The classes can be challenging
if you make them challenging.

00:37:40.140 --> 00:37:45.106
The social events, everything
is like a place where I mean for

00:37:45.106 --> 00:37:49.798
me helped me that I did know what
I wanted to do post the GSB.

00:37:49.798 --> 00:37:53.553
However, I recommend you
that get some ideas for

00:37:53.553 --> 00:37:59.415
what you want to get out of it because if
not, you're going to confront yourself

00:37:59.415 --> 00:38:04.925
with many opportunities and you're not
going to be able to do all of them.

00:38:04.925 --> 00:38:09.990
So, you're going to get anxiety,
stress and and all of that.

00:38:09.990 --> 00:38:12.570
So I wish I would have known that.

00:38:12.570 --> 00:38:17.978
You need to get there with more like
a focus otherwise it can be stressful.

00:38:21.876 --> 00:38:25.883
&gt;&gt; I think from you, and
the thing that most surprised me, and

00:38:25.883 --> 00:38:32.030
they say it very graphically at Stanford
in the beginning, is that we're all ducks.

00:38:32.030 --> 00:38:34.125
We look calm on the surface, but

00:38:34.125 --> 00:38:38.325
under the water we're all like
paddling really, really fast.

00:38:38.325 --> 00:38:43.786
And I think what most surprised me there
was that first quarter I struggled a lot,

00:38:43.786 --> 00:38:46.999
just as especially with my daughter and

00:38:46.999 --> 00:38:50.229
dealing with all
the intellectual challenge.

00:38:50.229 --> 00:38:54.804
I would get home and actually be happy
to be able to talk to people for

00:38:54.804 --> 00:38:57.924
two hours, not having said anything smart.

00:38:57.924 --> 00:39:02.773
And I think that once you
realize that everyone's in

00:39:02.773 --> 00:39:07.852
the same boat,
most people are paddling under water.

00:39:07.852 --> 00:39:12.776
You sort of relax and I think that's
the thing that most surprised me,

00:39:12.776 --> 00:39:18.466
realizing that most people were actually
the same and I think that surprised me.

00:39:21.504 --> 00:39:26.148
&gt;&gt; Thank you for that.

00:39:26.148 --> 00:39:29.946
I guess, Diego, you kind of touched on
what you would have done differently based

00:39:29.946 --> 00:39:30.949
on what you know now.

00:39:30.949 --> 00:39:36.280
Juan Manuel and an Alice is there anything
that you would have done differently

00:39:36.280 --> 00:39:41.465
based on some of those things that
surprised you about the GSB or otherwise?

00:39:41.465 --> 00:39:46.903
&gt;&gt; I only started taking courses
outside of the GSB in the second year.

00:39:46.903 --> 00:39:51.489
I maybe would have done it one quarter
earlier because I really like engineering

00:39:51.489 --> 00:39:56.087
and coding and things like that,
and so I ended up not being

00:39:56.087 --> 00:40:00.280
able to do one coding
class that I wanted to do.

00:40:00.280 --> 00:40:04.278
But I think that's the only thing
that I would have done differently,

00:40:04.278 --> 00:40:05.990
like look outside of the box.

00:40:05.990 --> 00:40:10.745
It really, he makes sure that I'm
taking in all of the Stanford

00:40:10.745 --> 00:40:16.765
because the GSB is immersed in a bigger
Stanford campus that I want to.

00:40:16.765 --> 00:40:18.955
That's the one thing that I think
I would have done differently.

00:40:18.955 --> 00:40:23.443
&gt;&gt; If you know I'm going to echo
what Diego say about professors,

00:40:23.443 --> 00:40:25.560
I didn't develop.

00:40:25.560 --> 00:40:29.410
Any strong relationships with professors,
I focus more on classmates.

00:40:29.410 --> 00:40:34.025
So looking back and talking to some
classmates that are very good friends,

00:40:34.025 --> 00:40:37.115
with professors even after
they have graduated.

00:40:37.115 --> 00:40:41.760
I wish that I will have identified
there is only a couple that I can still

00:40:41.760 --> 00:40:43.019
keep in touch, but

00:40:43.019 --> 00:40:48.325
I talked to some of my classmates that
are live with five different professors.

00:40:48.325 --> 00:40:50.935
And they went Go have coffee still or

00:40:50.935 --> 00:40:54.680
when they Professor comes
to Mexico they hang out.

00:40:54.680 --> 00:40:59.660
So I wish I will have taken
advantage of that great resource.

00:40:59.660 --> 00:41:01.370
That is the professors at the GSB.

00:41:03.990 --> 00:41:07.610
&gt;&gt; Thanks, thank you all for
sharing those reflections.

00:41:07.610 --> 00:41:12.381
We have a couple questions
about leadership style as well.

00:41:12.381 --> 00:41:17.028
Some of our attendees are curious how your

00:41:17.028 --> 00:41:22.474
leadership style changed
due to your experience

00:41:22.474 --> 00:41:27.667
at the GSB as well as how
did that affects your.

00:41:27.667 --> 00:41:33.505
I mean, how did that affect your
leadership in this very unprecedented

00:41:33.505 --> 00:41:42.514
time and COVID if it did
&gt;&gt; Now,

00:41:42.514 --> 00:41:47.190
I would say for me, I mean,
I think it did affect my leadership style.

00:41:47.190 --> 00:41:50.895
I think before that I didn't really
have that much leadership experience.

00:41:50.895 --> 00:41:54.405
Like I was in consulting,
I was still like a senior analyst.

00:41:54.405 --> 00:41:57.225
So I was just meeting two or three people.

00:41:57.225 --> 00:41:58.783
And then after that,

00:41:58.783 --> 00:42:03.977
I really felt like I was ready to
get more to lead bigger teams.

00:42:03.977 --> 00:42:06.145
I think the most important thing for

00:42:06.145 --> 00:42:09.830
me like takeaway from GSB has
been to really meet people where

00:42:09.830 --> 00:42:14.177
they are like understand that you
have different persons in your team.

00:42:14.177 --> 00:42:18.903
And they all did want different things and
really try to understand whether their

00:42:18.903 --> 00:42:22.780
personal motivations,
what is really important to them.

00:42:22.780 --> 00:42:26.994
Try to, like really meet their
development where they want to go and

00:42:26.994 --> 00:42:31.751
give them those that those challenges
that they really are interested in.

00:42:31.751 --> 00:42:36.226
Because if they're internally motivated,
they are much more productive, and

00:42:36.226 --> 00:42:39.670
you don't need to worry about
retaining them about like pay.

00:42:39.670 --> 00:42:43.080
Well, you still need to worry about that,
obviously, but I mean, it's easier.

00:42:43.080 --> 00:42:46.150
It's just much easier, and
you build a better team.

00:42:46.150 --> 00:42:49.719
Also, the other thing I think I would
say is one of the classes I had I don't

00:42:49.719 --> 00:42:50.764
remember which one.

00:42:50.764 --> 00:42:54.585
But it talks about If you're going to
make changes in your team, and

00:42:54.585 --> 00:42:58.614
I'm talking about when you take on
a new team, when you change jobs,

00:42:58.614 --> 00:43:01.272
make them once, and
cut deep, and cut once.

00:43:01.272 --> 00:43:04.060
I think that was what they said, I think
it was managing growing enterprise.

00:43:04.060 --> 00:43:09.010
I don't remember which wish it was, but I
think that's one of the takeaways I've had

00:43:09.010 --> 00:43:12.404
as well, like when you have
to make difficult decisions.

00:43:12.404 --> 00:43:16.298
Make them once and make them so
that you don't mess up the team so

00:43:16.298 --> 00:43:20.570
that people don't start worrying like
okay, how am I going to be next?

00:43:20.570 --> 00:43:22.870
Is it is this it or not?

00:43:22.870 --> 00:43:26.000
So I think that's been
one of my big takeaways.

00:43:26.000 --> 00:43:31.610
And I would say in COVID that helped, to
a certain extent, were in my previous job.

00:43:33.080 --> 00:43:37.092
At the end of my previous job,
we had to let go some people, so

00:43:37.092 --> 00:43:40.420
I would say that was really useful and
post COVID.

00:43:40.420 --> 00:43:44.886
I think one of the things that really
helps me now is that I try to innovate

00:43:44.886 --> 00:43:47.310
when I do meetings with my team.

00:43:47.310 --> 00:43:48.500
Like let's not use teams.

00:43:48.500 --> 00:43:52.281
Again, let's not use Zoom again,
let's use this other app that we can do or

00:43:52.281 --> 00:43:54.050
let's do something different.

00:43:54.050 --> 00:43:56.030
I send them coffees I do.

00:43:56.030 --> 00:44:01.668
And I think that constant innovation is
something that I really got from Stanford.

00:44:01.668 --> 00:44:04.180
&gt;&gt; That's awesome.

00:44:04.180 --> 00:44:08.595
&gt;&gt; For me, it has been,
I want it to be perfect before the GSB for

00:44:08.595 --> 00:44:11.980
everything that I do
when I was in consulting.

00:44:11.980 --> 00:44:15.290
And I worry too much
about these perfection.

00:44:15.290 --> 00:44:18.491
And after the GASB in a more
like emotional perfection,

00:44:18.491 --> 00:44:21.880
never showing if I was
breaking in siren anything.

00:44:21.880 --> 00:44:27.131
For the GSB that has changed a lot and
definitely more

00:44:27.131 --> 00:44:32.700
bigger I've noticed on notice
get to ask for n COVID.

00:44:32.700 --> 00:44:38.874
These became so high in the education
sector in communities where

00:44:38.874 --> 00:44:44.385
we work without asking for
help to my own teams to my board.

00:44:44.385 --> 00:44:50.917
To tell them I need more money like I'm
like the organization is in danger,

00:44:50.917 --> 00:44:55.593
so being able to express yourself and
be vulnerable.

00:44:55.593 --> 00:45:00.379
For me, it has been how my leadership
style has changed a lot and

00:45:00.379 --> 00:45:06.268
is in similar to Elise, I know that
people also have their vulnerabilities.

00:45:06.268 --> 00:45:11.608
And if they see the leader opening up,
you're going to know what is happening

00:45:11.608 --> 00:45:17.477
behind their mind, and he's going to help
you to identify how can you support them.

00:45:17.477 --> 00:45:22.460
To even become better at their job,
so definitely that's the way

00:45:22.460 --> 00:45:27.360
that the GSB changed me and
was like it's okay to be vulnerable.

00:45:27.360 --> 00:45:29.500
It's okay to show your emotions.

00:45:29.500 --> 00:45:34.355
And I'm not afraid anymore even if
people some people that are like

00:45:34.355 --> 00:45:38.880
me like I was before that
thought are showing emotions.

00:45:38.880 --> 00:45:43.140
Maybe it was a sign of weakness, I don't
care because maybe that's not the type of

00:45:43.140 --> 00:45:45.820
leader that I want to create
a strong relationship.

00:45:45.820 --> 00:45:51.101
So I want to create relationships with
people that are truly vulnerable and

00:45:51.101 --> 00:45:52.190
humane.

00:45:52.190 --> 00:45:55.770
So that has helped me a lot
to create even like now.

00:45:55.770 --> 00:46:00.708
Very good partnerships in the organization
because we connect with those people.

00:46:01.895 --> 00:46:09.760
&gt;&gt; I love all those points.

00:46:09.760 --> 00:46:14.109
Also, I thinking that I think a lot of
what happens at the GSV's that you get

00:46:14.109 --> 00:46:15.983
exposed to a lot of these ideas.

00:46:15.983 --> 00:46:20.315
Tools [INAUDIBLE] you just have to keep
practicing then the rest of your life,

00:46:20.315 --> 00:46:22.150
like snap, not going to come out.

00:46:22.150 --> 00:46:22.861
I was like,

00:46:22.861 --> 00:46:27.560
just I know kung fu like he was going to
you know have to keep practicing.

00:46:27.560 --> 00:46:31.916
For example, like a lot of the emotion
like connecting with your emotion's

00:46:31.916 --> 00:46:34.854
vulnerability, compassion,
self compassion.

00:46:34.854 --> 00:46:40.716
Pre GSB I was definitely very much
up here like post consulting,

00:46:40.716 --> 00:46:44.516
econ undergrad like a very analytical and

00:46:44.516 --> 00:46:49.450
I think I started opening
up more to these things.

00:46:49.450 --> 00:46:54.599
I think it helped me on the communication
side when pitching Lyla I remember

00:46:54.599 --> 00:47:00.091
getting the feedback those like I'm
hearing that you're excited about this.

00:47:00.091 --> 00:47:04.300
But you're speaking like a consultant,
that's a good point.

00:47:04.300 --> 00:47:10.159
So when I'm talking about lol I will lead
more of the emotion commands compassion.

00:47:10.159 --> 00:47:14.683
And self compassion super valuable
especially over the last few years both

00:47:14.683 --> 00:47:18.300
with myself and the team and
students and so on.

00:47:18.300 --> 00:47:21.720
So some both of those
they've definitely helped.

00:47:23.170 --> 00:47:27.753
I forget from which classes but at the
beginning of the pandemic we had to make

00:47:27.753 --> 00:47:32.637
a really hard choice around do we close
the program and fly all the students home.

00:47:32.637 --> 00:47:37.239
Do we keep them and hedge our bets since
one and remember this idea of like at

00:47:37.239 --> 00:47:41.471
the end of the day if you're in
a leadership position you have to make

00:47:41.471 --> 00:47:45.722
the final call you have to be decisive
kind of like what I was saying.

00:47:45.722 --> 00:47:50.330
But it's something you just have
to be decisive, make the call.

00:47:50.330 --> 00:47:54.710
But you can be inclusive,
you can listen and include others.

00:47:54.710 --> 00:47:58.998
So remember,
we set ourselves up 24 hours Timeline, so

00:47:58.998 --> 00:48:03.847
we're going to listen to the students,
the parents, the team, donors,

00:48:03.847 --> 00:48:08.631
advisors, everyone, but
this time tomorrow we're making the call.

00:48:08.631 --> 00:48:12.135
In hindsight,
that probably saved us from bankruptcy.

00:48:12.135 --> 00:48:17.171
[LAUGH] And then this connected to
leadership and entrepreneurship,

00:48:17.171 --> 00:48:22.548
I think I really came out of the GSB
having adopted the DNA of lean startup,

00:48:22.548 --> 00:48:26.455
experimentation, trial and
error, prototyping,

00:48:26.455 --> 00:48:30.143
also of empowering the team
to be the innovators.

00:48:30.143 --> 00:48:32.503
And I think we have come
out of the pandemic, but

00:48:32.503 --> 00:48:35.868
we are still in the pandemic, but
we're stronger now than before.

00:48:35.868 --> 00:48:40.031
We have virtual programs that
we had never considered before,

00:48:40.031 --> 00:48:42.394
a lot of that are team innovations.

00:48:42.394 --> 00:48:46.475
So, but being very comfortable
with trying new things,

00:48:46.475 --> 00:48:51.590
experimenting, being comfortable
with some things not working out.

00:48:51.590 --> 00:48:56.203
And again, empowering the team
to speak up, take ownership,

00:48:56.203 --> 00:49:01.166
innovate, collectively I think
over time lead to stronger teams,

00:49:01.166 --> 00:49:03.534
better organizations as well.

00:49:04.881 --> 00:49:07.571
Yeah, I think I credit
the GSB with a lot of that.

00:49:09.943 --> 00:49:11.255
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you.

00:49:11.255 --> 00:49:15.149
I think we have time for
maybe one more question and

00:49:15.149 --> 00:49:19.887
bringing together a couple
questions that I see in the Q&amp;A.

00:49:19.887 --> 00:49:26.955
A couple of our attendees are wondering,
when is the right time?

00:49:26.955 --> 00:49:31.092
How did you all know that it was
the right time to pursue an MBA?

00:49:31.092 --> 00:49:34.230
If you did know or
did you take the plunge not knowing?

00:49:34.230 --> 00:49:37.770
How did you know you were ready for
the experience?

00:49:43.902 --> 00:49:47.628
&gt;&gt; I think for me it was about,
I had to juggle two things,

00:49:47.628 --> 00:49:49.663
is it the right moment for me?

00:49:49.663 --> 00:49:53.002
Is it right moment for
my daughter and for me?

00:49:53.002 --> 00:49:59.554
It just coincided that I applied and
I got to just be at the average age,

00:49:59.554 --> 00:50:04.787
which is 27 or 28,
I entered the GSB when I was 28.

00:50:04.787 --> 00:50:08.962
But I started working when I was 22, so
I already had six years of experience, and

00:50:08.962 --> 00:50:12.082
it just coincided with my daughter
still being in preschool.

00:50:12.082 --> 00:50:16.257
[LAUGH] So for me that was important
because I wanted her to be in elementary

00:50:16.257 --> 00:50:20.592
school right away where she was going to
be the whole elementary school.

00:50:20.592 --> 00:50:25.711
So those two things combined,
having enough experience.

00:50:25.711 --> 00:50:30.955
I had a few classmates who
were just out of undergrad and

00:50:30.955 --> 00:50:34.607
maybe have taken one or two years off.

00:50:34.607 --> 00:50:39.046
And they had undoubtedly had also
a very good experience, but I remember

00:50:39.046 --> 00:50:43.585
them telling me, few of them telling me,
I wish I'd come a little later.

00:50:43.585 --> 00:50:48.057
So I could bring more to the class,
so I could give more back,

00:50:48.057 --> 00:50:52.293
because I feel like I'm only getting and
I'm not returning.

00:50:52.293 --> 00:50:54.407
And I think that is sort of true.

00:50:54.407 --> 00:50:58.258
I think you sort of need
a certain amount of not years, but

00:50:58.258 --> 00:51:02.591
a certain amount of experience to
really be able to contribute to

00:51:02.591 --> 00:51:05.574
the discussion that
goes on in every class.

00:51:11.373 --> 00:51:17.314
&gt;&gt; For me, [LAUGH] I remember I was
ready to submit my application and

00:51:17.314 --> 00:51:21.174
then I asked myself, okay, why the GSB?

00:51:21.174 --> 00:51:22.230
Why an MBA?

00:51:22.230 --> 00:51:23.693
Why the GSB?

00:51:23.693 --> 00:51:26.750
When I asked why now,
I didn't have a good answer for myself.

00:51:26.750 --> 00:51:31.791
I had done two years of consulting,
two years at a nonprofit.

00:51:31.791 --> 00:51:36.876
And I don't know why I had this idea of
two plus two plus two was a good idea,

00:51:36.876 --> 00:51:37.464
right?

00:51:37.464 --> 00:51:40.854
I was like if I leave this nonprofit now,

00:51:40.854 --> 00:51:46.057
when I graduate from the MBA,
I will be under 30 with an MBA.

00:51:46.057 --> 00:51:48.106
For some reason,
that's a good thing to be.

00:51:48.106 --> 00:51:56.196
[LAUGH] And for me, a couple of things
pushed me to wait one more year.

00:51:56.196 --> 00:52:02.160
One was that at this nonprofit,
I had built two years of knowledge,

00:52:02.160 --> 00:52:05.310
insight skills, social capital.

00:52:05.310 --> 00:52:09.487
So, I felt like I could give more to that
nonprofit if I stayed there one more year,

00:52:09.487 --> 00:52:11.378
third year instead of just leaving.

00:52:11.378 --> 00:52:15.912
And two, I didn't have almost
any managerial experience.

00:52:15.912 --> 00:52:21.014
So I tried to make sure I had that for
at least one year pre-MBA.

00:52:21.014 --> 00:52:27.688
I was at the GSB ages 28, 30, so
I was on the slightly older side.

00:52:27.688 --> 00:52:32.946
I feel I got a lot more out of
it than if I had gone earlier.

00:52:32.946 --> 00:52:38.668
Even now, after three years running,
I'm like, I wish I could go now.

00:52:38.668 --> 00:52:43.981
[LAUGH] I feel like I would get so
much more now just because, I don't know,

00:52:43.981 --> 00:52:49.655
you go through a class that touches on
hiring or a class that touches on firing.

00:52:49.655 --> 00:52:53.353
And now three years later,
I'm like, what did we cover?

00:52:53.353 --> 00:52:55.506
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:52:55.506 --> 00:52:58.299
&gt;&gt; Maybe I should have been a better

00:52:58.299 --> 00:52:59.854
student, yeah.

00:52:59.854 --> 00:53:02.703
&gt;&gt; In my case it was,
I guess, I'm the spectrum,

00:53:02.703 --> 00:53:04.856
I applied when I was 23 to the GSB.

00:53:04.856 --> 00:53:07.776
So I was one of the young ones,
but I started working at 20.

00:53:07.776 --> 00:53:11.256
In consultants,
I had three years of experience.

00:53:11.256 --> 00:53:15.161
For me, it was a sense of urgency
to wanting to do something for

00:53:15.161 --> 00:53:18.029
education in Latin America and
coming back.

00:53:18.029 --> 00:53:22.570
I grew up in a low socioeconomic
status of Mexico and

00:53:22.570 --> 00:53:27.638
coming back that people were
getting involved in crime,

00:53:27.638 --> 00:53:30.395
there was a lot of corruption.

00:53:30.395 --> 00:53:33.281
It was very unsafe to live in
the neighborhood that I was in.

00:53:33.281 --> 00:53:38.523
I was like, why am I the lucky one
living in Houston, having a great life.

00:53:38.523 --> 00:53:42.786
And that's when I was like I
need to find a way to speak,

00:53:42.786 --> 00:53:47.253
how can I get to a education
leadership position pastor?

00:53:47.253 --> 00:53:51.406
And identify that getting an MBA
at a top business school,

00:53:51.406 --> 00:53:56.170
combining with the education
knowledge will get me there faster.

00:53:56.170 --> 00:54:01.046
So that's what took me, and I knew it was
the right time because the problems that I

00:54:01.046 --> 00:54:03.248
was seeing in Mexico could not wait.

00:54:03.248 --> 00:54:08.031
So I was like, I need to make this quick,
so that's when I decided to apply.

00:54:08.031 --> 00:54:12.686
I got accepted when I was 22, I started
when I was 24, and I don't regret it.

00:54:12.686 --> 00:54:16.857
I definitely felt that I learned a lot,
but again,

00:54:16.857 --> 00:54:22.095
I come from this idea that I wanted
to make things happen faster.

00:54:22.095 --> 00:54:26.473
And it really helped me because
I don't think without the GSB,

00:54:26.473 --> 00:54:30.528
I will have become the executive
director of a nonprofit.

00:54:30.528 --> 00:54:35.811
Besides that, Mexico was when I joined
two years and a half, two years ago.

00:54:37.942 --> 00:54:41.727
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you all so
much for your candor, for

00:54:41.727 --> 00:54:45.524
sharing so
much of your journeys with our audience.

00:54:45.524 --> 00:54:51.507
I will note regarding the question
about how do you decide when to apply?

00:54:51.507 --> 00:54:56.837
Our community includes people with
a wide range of experiences and

00:54:56.837 --> 00:55:00.175
that includes years of work experience.

00:55:00.175 --> 00:55:03.844
As Alice mentioned, some folks might
have a couple years of experience,

00:55:03.844 --> 00:55:06.389
some folks might have over
a decade of experience,

00:55:06.389 --> 00:55:08.826
and all are welcome in
our application process.

00:55:08.826 --> 00:55:13.193
And we think that all of
those experiences bring

00:55:13.193 --> 00:55:18.775
a perspective that enriches
our educational environment.

00:55:18.775 --> 00:55:23.916
So thank you all again for
sharing your perspectives with us.

00:55:23.916 --> 00:55:27.065
For attendees,
we're going to throw a poll out there, so

00:55:27.065 --> 00:55:29.148
you can let us know how we're doing.

00:55:29.148 --> 00:55:31.929
These events are all about serving you, so

00:55:31.929 --> 00:55:37.274
we would love to get some of your feedback
about how this event has gone for you all.

00:55:37.274 --> 00:55:42.171
And thank you, again, for spending
a little bit of your day with us and

00:55:42.171 --> 00:55:43.006
take care.

00:55:43.006 --> 00:55:44.578
Thank you all so much.

00:55:44.578 --> 00:55:48.550
[MUSIC]

